---
import site from "../data/site.json";
// FOUR FIGURES, NO CAPTION, NO HAIRLINES (Dom, 2026-09-08). The "Who watches"
// lead-in column is gone — that is what the row gave up to go from five columns
// to four — and with it the framing that the strip is audience data: it now
// mixes proof (14%), the 7M credit, and audience fit. The fused left-edge
// hairlines between figures went in the same pass. Space separates the columns
// now, which is the same argument as the flat background: on a solid block a
// drawn line is depth the design already refused.
// Background: ONE FLAT COLOUR, see the style block.
---

<!-- NO HAIRLINE, TOP OR BOTTOM. The strip is a solid colour block: the colour
     change IS the divider at both edges, and a rule drawn on top of it is a
     line that has nothing to separate. The top rule went on 2026-09-07 (it was
     `border-y`; --color-line is a warm grey and the hero photo's bottom edge is
     near-black, so it read as a bright wire drawn across the picture and Dom
     circled it). The BOTTOM rule went on 2026-09-08 with the flattening: below
     it the page returns to the pale ground, and --color-line sits in value
     between the strip and the ground, so it read as a dirty grey seam under a
     saturated band. Full-value section changes do not need help. -->
<section aria-label="Results at a glance" class="stats-strip relative">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-(--container-page) px-6 py-10 sm:px-8">
    <dl class="stats-tiles relative grid grid-cols-2 items-start gap-x-8 gap-y-9 lg:grid-cols-4 lg:gap-x-10">
      {site.stats.map((stat) => (
        <div>
          <dd class="font-display text-stat text-strip-ink font-semibold">{stat.value}</dd>
          <dt class="text-strip-ink-dim mt-2 text-sm text-balance">{stat.label}</dt>
        </div>
      ))}
    </dl>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* The row has no CSS of its own any more. It was a captioned row (Dom,
     picker, 2026-09-07 — layout 5 of the strip board): a 200px lead-in column
     naming what the figures were for, each figure owning a left hairline so
     neighbours shared one line. Both are gone on Dom's call (2026-09-08), and
     the grid is four equal columns spaced by a gap, 2x2 on phones. Cells are
     top-aligned, not centred — the labels run to different line counts, and
     centring them would leave the four big figures sitting at four different
     heights. --color-strip-line in global.css now has no user.

     ── FLAT (Dom, 2026-09-08) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
     One colour. No gradient, no wash, no vignette, no texture — Dom: "no depth
     no nothing, we need to keep it simple."

     WHAT WAS HERE AND WHY IT IS GONE. Two layers, both removed on Dom's call:

       THE DUSK MESH — a dusk base under three radial gradients in the hero's
       own colours (sky blue low-left, sodium amber mid, navy top-right) plus an
       ink wash. It existed to make the strip read as the same photograph as the
       hero. A flat strip does not want to be a photograph, so the whole
       argument for it goes with it.

       THE SEAM — an extra 56px gradient at the top that darkened the strip to
       within ~20 levels of the hero photo's near-black bottom row, so the join
       between two dark images did not read as a cut. IT IS NOT NEEDED NOW AND
       CANNOT BE REINSTATED: it worked by darkening, and this strip is bright.
       There is no longer a near-match to preserve — the photo's dark bottom
       meets a bright colour, which is a full-value change and therefore reads
       as a deliberate section break rather than a mistake. That is what flat
       design wants at a join, and it is why nothing replaced the layer.

     The old public/stats-bg* render files remain unused (they were already
     dead after 2026-09-07).

     THE COLOUR ITSELF lives in --color-strip in global.css, along with the
     other four candidates and the reasoning. Change it there, not here — the
     ink and hairline are the same ink at three alphas and follow it. */
  .stats-strip {
    background-color: var(--color-strip);
  }
</style>
